---
// A lab page, laid out the way a lecture page is.
//
// The two used to diverge for no reason a reader could see. Lectures had
// already been through several rounds of this work and had settled on three
// layers: a title with a single quiet line of metadata under it, the lesson,
// and one clearly separated block of apparatus at the foot. Labs still opened
// with a subtitle, a bold date on a line of its own, and a four cell grid of
// METHOD, TAKES APART, BUILDS ON and RISK, so a reader met four typographic
// registers before the first sentence of the lesson. This uses BaseLayout and
// the same three layers.
//
// BaseLayout rather than ContentLayout for the same reason the lecture route
// gives: ContentLayout renders `description` twice, once as the meta
// description and once as a visible lead paragraph, with no way to keep the
// first without the second. The description is still doing its real jobs, on
// the session cards and in search results, and on a lab it now also opens the
// page, under a label, which is not the same thing as a subtitle: see below.
//
// What a lab opens with, and why in this order.
//
// The ask was that a week say what it is about and then list what the student
// will actually do, and that both come first. Both already existed and neither
// was doing that job. `description` is a one sentence statement of the week and
// was rendered nowhere a reader could see it. `spec` is the list of what the
// student must bring, and it was at the foot of the page among the apparatus,
// which is the last place a reader looks and the right place for a reference
// list but the wrong one for a set of instructions. So nothing here is new
// writing: the summary is `description` and the task list is `spec`, moved.
//
// They are not adjacent, and that is the one deliberate departure from what was
// asked. Rule 1 outranks the layout, and the safety block has to be met before
// a student starts, not found later. Measured at 390px on weeks 7 and 9, with
// the nav at 220px and a title that runs to three lines: the summary and the
// task list together are 533px, which puts "Before you run this" at 959px on an
// 844px screen, off the bottom entirely. The summary alone is 145px and leaves
// the safety heading and the whole of its stopping rule on the first screen.
//
// The split is also the better reading order, which is what settled it rather
// than the pixels. Week 7's stopping rule says to go back to your usual intake
// if you get a headache that will not shift, and that sentence means nothing
// until you know the week is about caffeine: the summary is what tells you. And
// the task list is what a student acts on, so it belongs after the rule for
// when to stop rather than before it. On the low risk weeks there is no safety
// block at all, so the summary and the task list are adjacent there and are
// exactly the first two things under the metadata line.
//
// Both blocks take the apparatus label, small and uppercase, from
// src/styles/apparatus.css. Quiet on purpose. The safety block is the loudest
// thing on a lab page and nothing above it may compete with it.
//
// Risk is the one thing that could not simply move to the foot with the rest
// of the old grid. Rule 1 says every week states how risky its experiment is,
// and on week 9 the word "medium" appeared exactly once in the whole built
// page: in that grid. spec/content-invariants.test.ts reads risk from the
// course API rather than from the HTML, so deleting the grid would have left
// every check green while the reader lost the fact. It is on the metadata line
// under the title now, which is earlier and more prominent than where it was.
import { render } from "astro:content";
import { getPublishedCollection } from "astro-course-university/content";
import RelatedContent from "astro-course-university/components/RelatedContent.astro";
import SpecList from "astro-course-university/components/SpecList.astro";
import BaseLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Claims from "../../components/Claims.astro";
import Protocol from "../../components/Protocol.astro";
import Safety from "../../components/Safety.astro";
import TeachingTeam from "../../components/TeachingTeam.astro";
import { formatCourseDate } from "../../lib/dates";
import { readingMinutes } from "../../lib/reading-time";
import SiteChrome from "../../components/SiteChrome.astro";
import { graphCollections, sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const sessions = await getPublishedCollection("sessions");
  return sessions.map((session) => ({ params: { slug: session.id }, props: { session } }));
}

const { session } = Astro.props;
const { Content } = await render(session);
const title = `Week ${session.data.week} ${sessionLabels.singular}: ${session.data.title}`;
const minutes = readingMinutes(session.body ?? "");
const risk = session.data.risk;

// "med" is a storage value, not a word to print at a reader.
const riskWord = risk === "med" ? "medium" : risk;

// Every week's description is written without a full stop, because until now
// its only jobs were the blurb on a session card and the <meta description>,
// and a card blurb does not take one. As the opening sentence of the page it
// does: a paragraph that stops without punctuation reads as a sentence that got
// cut off. Added here rather than in the twelve content files, so the card and
// the search result keep the form they were written for.
const summary = session.data.description?.trim();
const summarySentence = summary
  ? /[.!?:]$/.test(summary)
    ? summary
    : `${summary}.`
  : undefined;

// Matches the name ContentLayout derives, so the view transition from the lab
// card on the index page still lands on this heading.
const transitionName = `card-title-${Astro.url.pathname.replace(/^\/|\/$/g, "").replace(/\//g, "-")}`;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={session.data.description ?? undefined} {...siteConfig}>
  <SiteChrome />
  <h1 transition:name={transitionName}>{title}</h1>

  <p class="page-meta">
    <span>{formatCourseDate(session.data.date)}</span>
    <span>{minutes} min read</span>
    {
      risk && (
        <span class:list={["risk", `risk-${risk}`]}>
          <span class="risk-marker" aria-hidden="true" />
          {`${riskWord} risk`}
        </span>
      )
    }
  </p>

  {
    summarySentence && (
      <section class="week-summary" aria-labelledby="this-week">
        <h2 id="this-week">This week</h2>
        <p>{summarySentence}</p>
      </section>
    )
  }

  <Safety week={session} />

  <div class="lab-brief">
    <SpecList spec={session.data.spec} heading="What you bring to the lab">
      The lesson below is how you get there. The lab is where you show it.
    </SpecList>
  </div>

  <Content />

  <div class="apparatus">
    <Claims claims={session.data.claims} />
    <Protocol week={session} />
    <TeachingTeam teachers={session.data.teachers} />
    <RelatedContent entry={session} collections={graphCollections} />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The summary sentence sits tight under its own label and carries no styling
     of its own: body size, body colour, the same as every other sentence on the
     page. That is what keeps it a summary rather than a subtitle. A lead
     paragraph set larger or greyer under the title is the thing the lab layout
     got rid of, and it would be back the moment this sentence were given a
     typographic register nothing else on the page uses. */
  .week-summary p {
    margin-block: 0;
  }

  /* Where the orientation ends and the lesson begins.
     Without it the last line of the task list and the first line of the lesson
     sat one paragraph gap apart at the same size, so a reader could not see
     that one had stopped and the other had started: on week 1 the list ran
     straight into "This week we record a day and change nothing." A hairline in
     the theme's divider token is the same thing the apparatus draws between its
     sections at the foot, which is the point. A lab is three layers now, and
     each division is drawn the same way.

     The wrapper exists because the rule has to hang off something. SpecList is
     a package component, and an Astro-scoped selector reaching into it would be
     written .lab-brief[cid] .spec-list[cid], which nothing matches: the
     component's elements carry their own scope, not this page's. A div in this
     template does carry it.

     The gap is 27px on both sides: the list's own 1em bottom margin plus a
     small pad above the line, and --at-spacing-lg below it. The margin does not
     collapse through, because the border stops it. */
  .lab-brief {
    margin-block-end: var(--at-spacing-lg);
    padding-block-end: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
  }

  /* Risk is the one value on the metadata line a reader is scanning for, and
     it carries three channels rather than one, because a reader who cannot
     separate the theme's warm gold from the theme's warm amber, or who is
     reading a greyscale print, still has to be able to tell a high risk week
     from a low one.
     Weight is the first: the level is semibold where the date and the reading
     time are not. The marker is the second, and it is a shape rather than a
     colour: nothing at low, a hollow triangle at medium, a solid one at high.
     Colour is the third, and deliberately --at-warning rather than the brand
     gold, because it is the colour of the heading of the safety block further
     down that says what to do about it.
     The marker is a child element and not a ::before on the risk span. The
     separator middots on this line are already ::before on every child after
     the first, and an element has only one. */
  .risk {
    color: var(--at-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .risk-med,
  .risk-high {
    color: var(--at-warning);
  }

  .risk-marker::before {
    margin-inline-end: 0.3em;
  }

  .risk-med .risk-marker::before {
    content: "▵";
  }

  .risk-high .risk-marker::before {
    content: "▲";
  }
</style>
